    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d566">

                <pb facs="0561" n="477" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg477"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 855.48/728</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-21T15:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 21, 1918, 3 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4.30 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9631. Your 7226, 11th. My 9507, April 13, 11 p.m. I have just received the
                    following note from the Foreign Office: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>The Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs presents his compliments to
                            the United States Ambassador and with reference to His Excellency’s note
                            of the 12th instant relative to a question of allocating 60,000 tons of
                            shipping either for the transportation of United States troops and
                            supplies to France or to the Belgian Relief, has the honor to inform Mr.
                            Page that in replying as above it was assumed <pb facs="0562" n="478"
                                xml:id="pg478"/> that the 60,000 tons of shipping referred to in the
                            telegram from the Secretary of State in Washington was a particular
                            block of tonnage allocated for the carriage of urgent military supplies.
                            In these circumstances His Majesty’s Government did not feel [they
                            ought] to take the responsibility of urging the allocation of this
                            tonnage to Belgian Relief, although of course it is earnestly hoped that
                            the United States Government observes [<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >will</hi>], in addition to meeting their Army supply programme, be able
                            also to provide the necessary assistance in tonnage to enable the
                            Belgian Relief carrier [<hi rendition="#italic">to carry</hi>] the
                            minimum requirements of food-stuff, et cetera, to Rotterdam, and in fact
                            a request to this effect has recently been sent from Belgian Relief in
                            London to America.</p>
                        <p>Mr. Balfour would be most grateful if Mr. Page would be so good as to
                            telegraph to the United States Government in the above sense in order
                            that there may be no misunderstanding on the part of the United States
                            Government as to the attitude which His Majesty’s Government are taking
                            in regard to tonnage assistance for the Belgian Relief Commission.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Page</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
